# Database

Wishly uses Prisma for database access.

The current development database is SQLite.

Example Prisma configuration:

```prisma
datasource db {
  provider = "sqlite"
  url      = "file:dev.sqlite"
}
```

---

## Wishlist Model

The current wishlist model is:

```prisma
model Wishlist {
  id         String   @id @default(cuid())
  customerId String   @unique
  shop       String
  items      Json
  createdAt  DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt  DateTime @updatedAt
}
```

Each logged-in customer currently has one wishlist record.

Example:

```json
{
  "customerId": "9259301699819",
  "shop": "example.myshopify.com",
  "items": [
    {
      "productId": "9105835557099",
      "handle": "vanilla-candle",
      "addedAt": "2026-09-06T20:43:21.047Z"
    }
  ]
}
```

---

# Wishlist API

The wishlist API route is currently:

```text
/api/wishlist
```

It supports retrieving and saving logged-in customer wishlist data.

---

## GET Wishlist

Example request:

```text
GET /api/wishlist?customerId=9259301699819
```

Example response:

```json
{
  "success": true,
  "message": "Wishlist retrieved successfully",
  "wishlist": {
    "id": "wishlist_id",
    "customerId": "9259301699819",
    "shop": "example.myshopify.com",
    "items": [
      {
        "productId": "9105835557099",
        "handle": "vanilla-candle",
        "addedAt": "2026-09-06T20:43:21.047Z"
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

If the customer does not have a wishlist yet, the API returns an empty items array.

---

## POST Wishlist

Example request:

```text
POST /api/wishlist
```

Body:

```json
{
  "customerId": "9259301699819",
  "shop": "example.myshopify.com",
  "items": [
    {
      "productId": "9105835557099",
      "handle": "vanilla-candle",
      "addedAt": "2026-09-06T20:43:21.047Z"
    }
  ]
}
```

The API uses Prisma `upsert()` so that the customer's wishlist is either created or updated.

---

# Storefront Wishlist API

The Theme App Extension exposes a global JavaScript API:

```js
window.Wishly
```

Currently available methods include:

```js
window.Wishly.getWishlist();

window.Wishly.getGuestWishlist();

window.Wishly.getCustomerWishlist();

window.Wishly.addToWishlist(
  productId,
  handle
);

window.Wishly.removeFromWishlist(
  productId
);

window.Wishly.toggleWishlist(
  productId,
  handle
);

window.Wishly.updateButtons();

window.Wishly.updateCounter();
```

You can also check the current customer state:

```js
window.Wishly.isLoggedIn
```

---

# Product Wishlist Button

Products can expose a wishlist button using data attributes.

Example:

```liquid
<button
  type="button"
  class="wishora-heart-button"
  data-wishora-product="{{ product.id }}"
  data-wishora-handle="{{ product.handle }}"
  aria-label="Add {{ product.title | escape }} to wishlist"
>
  <span class="wishora-heart">♡</span>
</button>
```

Wishly automatically detects buttons containing:

```text
data-wishora-product
```

The button changes between:

```text
♡
```

and:

```text
♥
```

depending on whether the product exists in the wishlist.

---

# Wishlist Counter

Wishlist counters can use:

```html
<span data-wishora-counter>0</span>
```

Wishly automatically updates elements containing:

```text
data-wishora-counter
```

when the wishlist changes.

---

# Wishlist Page

The Theme App Extension also contains a wishlist page section.

The page retrieves wishlist data using:

```js
const wishlist =
  await window.Wishly?.getWishlist() || [];
```

Because `getWishlist()` is asynchronous, it must be called using `await`.

For guest customers:

```text
getWishlist()
    ↓
localStorage
```

For logged-in customers:

```text
getWishlist()
    ↓
GET /api/wishlist
    ↓
Prisma Database
```

---

# Removing Wishlist Items

Removing an item is also asynchronous:

```js
await window.Wishly.removeFromWishlist(
  productId
);
```

For guests, this modifies localStorage.

For logged-in customers, this updates the wishlist stored in the application database.

---

# Running the App Locally

Install dependencies:

```bash
npm install
```

Generate the Prisma client:

```bash
npx prisma generate
```

Run Prisma migrations:

```bash
npx prisma migrate dev
```

Start the Shopify development environment:

```bash
shopify app dev
```

Shopify CLI will start the application and Theme App Extension development environment.

---

# Prisma Commands

Create a migration:

```bash
npx prisma migrate dev --name migration_name
```

Generate Prisma Client:

```bash
npx prisma generate
```

Check migration status:

```bash
npx prisma migrate status
```

Open Prisma Studio:

```bash
npx prisma studio
```

Prisma Studio can be useful for inspecting customer wishlist records during development.

---

# Development API URL

During local development, the storefront currently communicates with the application through the Shopify CLI/Cloudflare development tunnel.

For example:

```js
const API_URL =
  "https://your-current-tunnel.trycloudflare.com/api/wishlist";
```

The Cloudflare tunnel URL may change after restarting:

```bash
shopify app dev
```

If wishlist API requests suddenly stop working, verify that `wishlist.js` contains the current development application URL.

This hard-coded tunnel URL is temporary and should be replaced with a production-safe routing strategy before release.

---

# Current Wishlist Flow

## Guest adds a product

```text
Customer clicks heart
        ↓
toggleWishlist()
        ↓
addToWishlist()
        ↓
localStorage
        ↓
Update heart
        ↓
Update counter
```

---

## Logged-in customer adds a product

```text
Customer clicks heart
        ↓
toggleWishlist()
        ↓
GET existing wishlist
        ↓
Add product
        ↓
POST updated wishlist
        ↓
Prisma Database
        ↓
Update heart
        ↓
Update counter
```

---

## Logged-in customer opens wishlist page

```text
Wishlist Page
      ↓
getWishlist()
      ↓
getCustomerWishlist()
      ↓
GET /api/wishlist
      ↓
Prisma
      ↓
Render wishlist
```

---

# Planned Wishlist Synchronization

The intended login synchronization flow is:

```text
Guest Wishlist
localStorage
      ↓
Customer logs in
      ↓
Fetch account wishlist
      ↓
Merge guest + account wishlist
      ↓
Remove duplicates
      ↓
Save merged wishlist
      ↓
Clear guest wishlist
```

This functionality is planned and is not yet fully implemented.

---

# Planned Logout Behavior

The desired behavior is:

```text
Account wishlist:

[A, B, C]

      ↓ Logout

Guest receives local snapshot:

[A, B, C]

      ↓

Guest removes A

Guest localStorage:

[B, C]

Database remains:

[A, B, C]

      ↓ Login again

Account wishlist:

[A, B, C]
```

Guest actions after logout must not modify the customer's permanent database wishlist.

This requires an account wishlist snapshot/cache strategy and will be implemented separately.

---

# Customer Metafields

Shopify Customer metafield synchronization has been considered for Wishly.

The intended future architecture could be:

```text
Logged-in Customer
        ↓
Wishly API
       ↙ ↘
App Database
Shopify Customer Metafield
```

Customer metafield synchronization is **not currently part of the active implementation**.

The application database is currently the source of truth for logged-in customer wishlists.

---

# Important Security Notice

The current wishlist API accepts a `customerId` supplied by storefront JavaScript.

For example:

```text
/api/wishlist?customerId=9259301699819
```

This is acceptable only for the current development/testing stage.

It must **not** be considered secure customer authentication.

Before production/App Store release, the application must verify storefront customer identity server-side and prevent customers from retrieving or modifying another customer's wishlist by changing a customer ID.

The production version should use an appropriate Shopify-supported authenticated storefront flow and stricter CORS/API validation.

---

# Production TODO

Before submitting Wishly to the Shopify App Store, the project still needs production hardening and additional functionality, including:

* Secure storefront customer authentication
* Remove trust in browser-supplied customer IDs
* Production database such as PostgreSQL
* Replace hard-coded development tunnel URL
* Restrict CORS
* Guest-to-customer wishlist migration
* Logout wishlist snapshot behavior
* Better API error handling
* Request validation
* Shop/customer isolation
* Loading states
* Empty/error states
* Product information on wishlist page
* Product images
* Product prices
* Product links
* Variant handling where required
* Pagination or wishlist limits where appropriate
* App uninstall cleanup
* GDPR/privacy compliance
* Shopify mandatory privacy webhooks
* Production logging
* Rate-limit/error handling
* App settings
* Merchant onboarding
* Theme compatibility testing
* App Store submission review

